{"id":822,"date":"2020-11-06T12:15:57","date_gmt":"2020-11-06T20:15:57","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=822"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:33","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:33","slug":"gyroscope","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/gyroscope\/","title":{"rendered":"4D: Gyroscope"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1be21\" name=\"6a22167a1be21\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a gyroscope?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you think of a gyroscope, you may be thinking of a mounted, spinning wheel that stays balanced in the same orientation, no matter which direction you turn or flip the gyroscope. How? This can be explained by conservation of momentum. While you may not have heard of this term before, you have experienced this. For example, if you have ever spun around on an office chair, you probably noticed you spin faster when you bring your arms and legs in. Since momentum has to be conserved, your speed increases when you bring your mass (arms and legs) closer to you.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1971&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In electronics, gyroscopes don\u2019t look like this. They are tiny chips that also use motion to detect changes in orientation. We know it&#8217;s hard to believe, but the tiny microchips you see on the CoDrone Mini have moving parts that are affected by rotations. These changes can be measureed.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c015\" name=\"6a22167a1c015\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How does it work?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gyro sensors are a lot like compasses &#8212; they sense rotational motion and detect orientation to figure out what direction something is facing. They&#8217;re used in everything from space shuttles to cell phones, and now your CoDrone Mini! Gyroscopes are necessary to stabilize your drone while flying since many factors could cause your drone to drift off course. A gyroscope can measure how far and in which direction your drone is tilting or rotating. Using some math and <\/span><b>algorithms<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, a drone can try and correct any unwanted <\/span><b>displacements<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> to remain stable. Displacement is a movement from one position to another position.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In short, it keeps CoDrone Mini from getting dizzy![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c0a6\" name=\"6a22167a1c0a6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting gyroscope angles<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">There are three orientations that we are concerned with when we are flying a drone for stability: roll, pitch, and yaw. The gyro sensor in your CoDrone will return values from 0 to 180 degrees, positive or negative, that correspond to the degrees the drone rotated in each axis. If you have trouble remembering which way is positive and which way is negative, think of the flight movements!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_images_carousel images=&#8221;642,643,644&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Did you notice that throttle is not included? When a drone flies straight up or down, the change in position is not an <\/span><b>angular<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> (turning) displacement but a vertical displacement. The gyroscope does not measure vertical displacement. That&#8217;s a job for the height sensor!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c13f\" name=\"6a22167a1c13f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting gyroscope angles<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Turn on your drone and place it flat on a table. Then, use the function <em>get_angle()<\/em> and print out the results. What do you notice?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nangle  = drone.get_angle()\r\nprint(angle)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Since there are three axes, the function will return a list object that contains the degrees that CoDrone Mini is tilting in the roll, pitch, and yaw directions. This is why you are seeing three numbers! You can also access the angles individually like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nangle  = drone.get_angle()\r\n\r\nprint(angle.ROLL)\r\nprint(angle.PITCH)\r\nprint(angle.YAW)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c1cd\" name=\"6a22167a1c1cd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Put it in a loop!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is not helpful to take one snapshot of the angle data because you can&#8217;t see how the angles change. Set up a<strong> for loop<\/strong> that will read the drone\u2019s angles 900 times. This sounds like a lot, but this will give enough time to rotate the drone in every direction to pick up its angle readings.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(900):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0angle\u00a0 = drone.get_angle()\r\n\u00a0\u00a0print(angle)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>When you run your code, your drone will not fly so try picking it up and moving it in different directions slowly to see the numbers change. You will notice that sometimes the numbers are positive and sometimes they are negative. Remember that left roll, backward pitch, or left yaw will result in negative degrees. When you turn beyond 180 degrees, the value will switch to -180.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c23b\" name=\"6a22167a1c23b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Turn 90 degrees<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You\u2019ve probably noticed that you can\u2019t program your drone to fly a specific amount of degrees. Up until now, you could only use trial and error with the power and number of seconds. Sometimes this method is great, but for things that need precise angles, like flying in a shape, it doesn\u2019t work well.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fear not! With your new knowledge about CoDrone Mini\u2019s gyroscope, you can create a function that will have the drone turn a specific number of degrees to the right or left. It uses information from the gyro sensor to make more accurate turns.\u00a0\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Let&#8217;s start with a 90-degree turn (perfect for squares!). Think about how you would write code to keep turning <em>while<\/em> the angle reading is still less than <em>90<\/em>.\u00a0<strong>Hint:<\/strong> You need to use a while loop to check the yaw angle.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<pre class=\"enlighter-clipboard\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.reset_sensor() # This makes sure the gyro starts at 0!\r\nangle = drone.get_angle()\r\nwhile angle.YAW &lt; 90:\r\n  drone.set_yaw(-20) # Yaw is actually opposite! \r\n  drone.move() # No parameters since we need to move \"forever\"\r\n  angle  = drone.get_angle() # Update the angle\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\nprint(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c2d5\" name=\"6a22167a1c2d5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Gyro with LED<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your challenge for this part: write a program that will change CoDrone Mini\u2019s LED colors as the drone turns! You can pick whatever colors you like, but we used:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Red for 0-90 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Yellow for 90-180 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Green for -180 to -90 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Blue for -90 to 0 degrees<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, import the necessary libraries, create the drone object, and pair the drone. Next, add a sleep command for 1 second and reset the drone\u2019s sensors.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add a for loop that repeats 100 times and will get the drone\u2019s yaw with the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_angle()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function and then store it to a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">angle<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, write an if statement that will turn the CoDrone Mini\u2019s LED red if the angle is greater than 0 and less than 90. Be careful with this one! Negative numbers are being used in this program so you do need to include greater than 0 in this conditional.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Do the same for the other three angle settings, but use else if statements instead. Finally, include an else statement that will turn the drone\u2019s LED white.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Include a sleep command for .05 seconds and then write a print command that will display as \u201cDone!\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Note: this program doesn\u2019t include any takeoff, landing, or flight commands! As a challenge, add those in yourself.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.reset_sensor()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(100):\r\n\r\n    angle = drone.get_angle().YAW\r\n\r\n    if angle &gt; 0 and angle &lt; 90:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.LED_color(255,0,0,100) # red\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif angle &gt; 90 and angle &lt; 180:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(255,255,0,100) # yellow\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif angle &gt; -180 and angle &lt; -90:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(0,255,0,100) # green\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif angle &gt; -90 and angle &lt; 0:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(0,0,255,100) # blue\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.LED_color(255,255,255,100) # white\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Done!\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c33c\" name=\"6a22167a1c33c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Turn_degree()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To fly in shape formations or do autonomous flight missions, you will be using the gyroscope a lot. Instead of having to rewrite the turning code over and over, turn it into a function <em>turn_right_degree(degrees)<\/em> that will take a parameter of how many degrees you want to turn right.\u00a0<strong>Remember that the gyroscope uses a positive angle for turning left and a negative angle for turning right. This is opposite the flight movements!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def turn_right_degrees(degrees):\r\n    \r\n    drone.reset_sensor()\r\n    angle = drone.get_angle()\r\n    while angle.YAW &gt; -90:\r\n        drone.set_yaw(20)\r\n        drone.move()\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\nturn_right_degrees(45)\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>Try changing the parameters for <em>turn_right_degrees()<\/em>. Make sure you stay below 180.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Challenge:<\/strong> Write a function <em>turn_left_degrees()<\/em> all on your own![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167a1c39f\" name=\"6a22167a1c39f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: A More Perfect Polygon<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use the gyro and loops to have your drone fly in the shape of your choosing!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Triangle<\/li>\n<li>Square<\/li>\n<li>Pentagon<\/li>\n<li>Hexagon<\/li>\n<li>Octagon<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 What is a gyroscope? [vc_column_text]When you think of a gyroscope, you may be thinking of a mounted, spinning wheel that stays balanced in the same orientation, no&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/gyroscope\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1729,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2125,2162,2161,2156,2122,2134,2155],"class_list":["post-822","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-gyro","lesson-tag-gyroscope","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensor","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/822","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":9,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/822\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":8819,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/822\/revisions\/8819"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1729"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=822"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=822"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=822"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}